\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Wilson currents as affine dual defects}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For the \vocabk{cyclic lattice data}{fdip-00NL}, let
#{J\in Z_1(K;A)} be a closed current. Its Wilson insertion is
#{\zeta^{\langle J,a\rangle}}, with unnormalized numerator
##{Z[J]=N^{-n_1}\sum_{a\in C^1(K;A)}
 \zeta^{\langle J,a\rangle}\prod_p w_p((\covwedge a)_p).}
It is gauge invariant and satisfies
##{Z[J]=\sum_{\substack{b\in C_2(K;A)\\\partial b=-J}}
                         \prod_p\widehat w_p(b_p).}
In particular #{Z[J]=0} if #{[J]\ne0} in #{H_1(K;A)}.
If #{J} is a boundary, choose #{b_0} with #{\partial b_0=-J}.
Using #{r}, #{h_s} and #{\widehat W} from \ref{fdip-00NO},
##{Z[J]=\frac1{|Z^{r-1}(K^*;A)|}
  \sum_{s\in H^r(K^*;A)}
  \sum_{\alpha\in C^{r-1}(K^*;A)}
    \widehat W(D_2b_0+\covwedge\alpha+h_s).}
The affine shift obeys the Bianchi-defect equation
##{\covwedge(D_2b_0)=-D_1J.}
This full-sector expression is independent of the spanning
chain #{b_0}. The normalized expectation #{Z[J]/Z} is
defined when #{Z} is a unit of #{R}. The changed-weight
interpretation is related to
\citet{Section 7.1, pp. 15–16, Equations (66)–(68)}{wegner2014duality};
the signs and independence assertion follow below.}

\proof{
\p{For a vertex cochain #{\lambda},
#{\langle J,\covwedge\lambda\rangle
=\langle\partial J,\lambda\rangle=0}, proving gauge
invariance. Fourier expansion as in \ref{fdip-00NO} changes
the exponent to #{\langle\partial b+J,a\rangle}.
Link orthogonality therefore imposes #{\partial b=-J}.
A solution exists exactly when #{J} is a boundary; every
solution is #{b_0+z} for a unique #{z\in Z_2}.
Applying the same dual-cycle parameterization gives the
affine formula with its unchanged multiplicity. The sign
in \ref{fdip-00NM} gives
#{\covwedge D_2b_0=D_1\partial b_0=-D_1J}.}

\p{For a second choice #{b'_0=b_0+c}, one has #{\partial c=0}.
Write #{D_2c=\covwedge\beta+h_t}. Representatives need not
be additive: for every #{s}, choose #{\gamma_s} satisfying
#{h_s+h_t-h_{s+t}=\covwedge\gamma_s}, which is possible
because the two sides have the same cohomology class.
The new argument then equals
##{D_2b'_0+\covwedge\alpha+h_s
 =D_2b_0+\covwedge(\alpha+\beta+\gamma_s)+h_{s+t}.}
The map #{(s,\alpha)\mapsto
(s+t,\alpha+\beta+\gamma_s)} is a bijection of the full
parameter set. It proves spanning independence, including
changes with nonzero cohomology class. If #{c=\partial u},
then #{D_2c=-\covwedge D_3u}, already an ordinary shift of
#{\alpha}.}
}

\p{The unit qualification is essential over a general ring.
In #{R=\mathbb C\times\mathbb C} with the valid diagonal
root from \ref{fdip-00NK}, constant weights #{w_p=(1,0)}
on every face give #{Z=(1,0)}. This element is nonzero
but has no multiplicative inverse.}
